# MAGIC TRAILING COMMA — a `,` INSIDE A COMMENT is not an author comma.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture's SUBJECT is fmt
# output.
#
# THE CELL HAS ZERO CORPUS WITNESSES. It exists because the comma probe runs on
# SUB-formatters too — `element_to_string` builds those with an EMPTY comment
# table BY DESIGN, so any comment-table lookup is VACUOUS there. A probe that
# asks the table whether a byte is inside a comment gets "no" for every byte of
# every comment, and a `,` written inside one reads as the author's.
#
# RED, break-and-watch (the probe has no pre-fix behaviour to red against):
# re-implementing `author_trailing_comma` as the comment-table-aware WINDOW
# scan the first prototype used turns the chain below into
#
#     int n = xs
#         .map((int v): v * 2)
#         .filter(
#             (int v): (v
#                 > g(
#                     1,        <- a comma NOBODY wrote
#                 )))
#         .len()
#
# i.e. a one-line chain cascaded into seven. Restored, the chain stays on ONE
# line and no comma appears. This file is NOT a fixed point: the chain packs and
# the comment leaves it (see the last paragraph), so the assertion is on the
# exact expected TEXT.
#
# ⚠ The ratified DECISIVE-BYTE contract is stronger than the window scan on
# this input in a second way: the first byte that is neither whitespace nor
# comment decides, so a `#` the probe fails to recognise TERMINATES the scan
# (answer: no comma) instead of letting it run on into the comment. That is why
# the self-contained `#`-to-EOL skip and the vacuous table give the SAME answer
# here, and why the window scan does not. The source pin
# `formatter_magic_comma_probe_is_self_contained` guards the other direction.
#
# The comment escaping the chain below is the SEPARATELY FILED pre-render-above
# defect, unchanged by this canon and not what this fixture is about.
int g(int a):
    return a

void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2, 3]
    int n = xs.map((int v): v * 2).filter((int v): v > g(
        1
        # a, b
    )).len()
    print(n)
